Chondrite Bashment
Ingredients: DANCEHALL + AMERICANA / ALTERNATIVE + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Marimba  
Creative Catalyst: Ancient horological escapements and fallen nickel-iron bolides are forged into kinetic, polyrhythmic sound-weapons.

Chondrite Bashment sounds like an illegal Kingston sound clash staged inside a failing deep-crater copper mine: jagged Appalachian slide-guitar laments and lonesome murder-ballad cadences are slammed into thunderous, half-time dembow sub-bass, all propelled by the icy, bell-like strike of celestial stone marimba bars and the frantic, ticking rattle of wind-up gear assemblies.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 94–106 BPM. A heavy, broken-shuffled dancehall dembow lurch underpinned by the syncopated, unspooling clatter of antique brass balance wheels and Appalachian clawhammer bounce.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Dorian and blues-scale mountain modes colliding with dystopian minor drones, punctuated by the unearthly, microtonal metallic overtones produced by rough-cut meteoric iron.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Escapement Gating: Sub-bass 808 hits are sidechained through mechanical ticking escapements that stutter and drop out irregularly like damaged gears.
  2. Meteorite Slapback: High-velocity mallets striking chondrite stone bars captured on degraded wire-recorders, creating a dry, glassy percussion transient followed by a hollow iron resonance.
  3. Acid-Well Toasting: Dual-channel vocal tracks pairing cavernous Jamaican-style patois toasting with deadpan, dust-choked Southern gothic storytelling, saturated through scorched tube limiters.
- Dynamics: Songs open with sparse, windblown mechanical ticking and ghostly bottleneck slide whimpers before dropping abruptly into cavernous, rib-caving bass pressure, ultimately culminating in runaway clockwork stampedes where mallets and gears run red-hot.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born along the dry riverbed of the Crater-Arroyo—a sunken, post-drought borderline trench where asteroid miners and displaced Caribbean deep-well drillers formed lawless scrap communes—the genre erupted from the ruins of scavenged horology shops. Stripping 19th-century railway clocks and grandfather chimes from buried rail hubs, outlaw soundmen bolted slabs of iridescent, nickel-iron meteorites onto brass pendulums to create "orrery-marimbas" that could cut through toxic dust storms. Gatherings happen inside flooded, subterranean concrete cisterns known as "Cistern Clashes," where youth wearing oilcloth duster coats lined with copper insect netting and jeweler's loupes dance in thick red dust, timing their footwork to the violent unwinding of mainsprings while performers deliberately short-circuit industrial fuse boxes to generate lighting and bass pops.

Typical Instruments
- The Orrery-Marimba: A massive wooden-and-brass frame suspending tuned keys made from chondritic meteorite shards, struck by clockwork-driven trip-hammers.
- Resonator Lap Steel: A rusted National style steel guitar fitted with ungrounded electromagnetic pickups made from copper telecommunications scrap.
- Clockwork Governor Percussion: Stripped pocket-watch escapements, chiming train chronometers, and loose gear teeth rattled inside kerosene tins.
- Gutter-Sub Synthesizers: Analog bass modules routed through long, corrugated iron drainage pipes for physical acoustic phase cancellation.
- Bowed Balance Springs: Coiled steel watch mainsprings bowed with horsehair to create keening, saw-toothed high frequencies.

